Subject: gfx_sdl2: on iOS the display link paces the frame, not a busy-wait

docs/pacing.md is the design; this is the one place in the engine it touches.

Upstream presents when `sync_framerate_with_timer()` says so - a `sysSleep`
followed by a `sysCpuRelax()` spin on the performance counter
(gfx_sdl2.cpp:329-361). On a phone that spin is a burnt core, heat, and, once
the thermal state leaves nominal, a LOWER sustained frame rate than doing
nothing would have given. Stacked under ANGLE's own `eglSwapInterval(1)` wait
it is also two clocks that do not agree, which is micro-stutter no profiling
run can explain because nothing is actually slow.

On iOS the frame therefore waits on a CADisplayLink running on its own thread
(app/ios/PDPacing.m) and presents exactly once per callback, with the ANGLE
swap interval set to 0 so nothing waits twice. The wait answers 0 when the app
is backgrounded, and then the present is skipped rather than swapped into a
CAMetalLayer drawable the system has taken back - the family's "looks like a
port bug, is actually a lifecycle bug" failure.

Nothing here changes the sim rate or `--fixed-step`, so the seeded replay is
untouched; a replay run bypasses the pacer entirely on the shell side.

Every other platform keeps upstream's limiter exactly as it was.

diff --git a/port/fast3d/gfx_sdl2.cpp b/port/fast3d/gfx_sdl2.cpp
index 4738da8a9..0eb8b0530 100644
--- a/port/fast3d/gfx_sdl2.cpp
+++ b/port/fast3d/gfx_sdl2.cpp
@@ -23,6 +23,11 @@ extern "C" int pdAngleSetSwapInterval(int interval);
 extern "C" int pdAngleGetSwapInterval(void);
 #endif
 
+#ifdef PLATFORM_IOS
+// app/ios/PDPacing.m — the display-link pacer. 1 = present now, 0 = skip.
+extern "C" int pdIosPacingWaitForPresent(void);
+#endif
+
 static SDL_Window* wnd;
 static SDL_GLContext ctx;
 static SDL_Renderer* renderer;
@@ -243,7 +248,13 @@ static void gfx_sdl_init(const struct GfxWindowInitSettings *set) {
     }
     sysLogPrintf(LOG_NOTE, "SDL: created GL3.0es context through ANGLE (%s)", pdAngleGetVendorString());
     pdAngleGetDrawableSize(&window_width, &window_height);
+#ifdef PLATFORM_IOS
+    // 0, not 1: the display link is the clock, and two waits stacked on two
+    // clocks is what micro-stutter is made of (docs/pacing.md).
+    pdAngleSetSwapInterval(0);
+#else
     pdAngleSetSwapInterval(1);
+#endif
     SDL_ShowWindow(wnd);
     qpc_freq = SDL_GetPerformanceFrequency();
     return;
@@ -462,6 +473,19 @@ static inline void sync_framerate_with_timer(void) {
 }
 
 static void gfx_sdl_swap_buffers_begin(void) {
+#ifdef PLATFORM_IOS
+    // CADisplayLink is the only pacer on iOS (docs/pacing.md). The desktop
+    // limiter above it is a sysCpuRelax() spin - a burnt core, heat, and a
+    // clock that does not agree with the display server's - so it does not run
+    // here at all. Instead the game thread blocks in the kernel until the link
+    // says the next frame is due, and presents once. A 0 answer means the app
+    // is backgrounded: skip the present rather than swap into a CAMetalLayer
+    // drawable the system has taken back.
+    if (!pdIosPacingWaitForPresent()) {
+        return;
+    }
+    pdAngleSwapBuffers();
+#else
     if (target_fps) {
         sync_framerate_with_timer();
     }
@@ -476,6 +500,7 @@ static void gfx_sdl_swap_buffers_begin(void) {
         SDL_GL_SwapWindow(wnd);
 #endif
     }
+#endif
 }
 
 static void gfx_sdl_swap_buffers_end(void) {
